  "slug": "optical-assisted-electro-2157",
  "summary": "Recent preprints demonstrate nonlinear and electro-optic light generation in new platforms, from cryogenic silicon to gain-assisted fiber resonators.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of resonant cavities to shape coherent light through nonlinear or modulated interactions. One work demonstrates telecom-band optical parametric oscillation in a passive silicon microring at 4.5 K under a single continuous-wave pump, with a reported 2.25 mW onset. Another uses a pair of gain-assisted electro-optic fiber ring resonators with intracavity modulation to produce a 1.8 THz flat-top comb for dual-comb spectroscopy. A third shows that intensity-modulated driving near mechanical resonance enlarges optomechanical oscillation amplitude toward the cavity-bandwidth limit and extends frequency comb bandwidth. The fourth, a 60-cm in-vacuum Zerodur cavity, addresses cavity stability for optical lattice atom interferometry rather than light generation. What is new is the demonstration of these processes in specific platforms: cryogenic passive silicon, gain-assisted electro-optic fiber cavities, and modulated optomechanical systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
